# ── Source-code line endings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalize all source files to LF on commit/checkout. This prevents the
# CRLF↔LF drift that bloated the proxy.py diff in PR #594 by ~4000 lines of
# pure line-ending noise (every line "deleted" and re-added with different
# endings). Windows contributors will see LF inside their editors, which is
# what the rest of the toolchain (pytest, ruff, mypy, bandit, esbuild) expects.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Explicit overrides for files that MUST be platform-native ───────────────
# Windows shell scripts and PowerShell files need CRLF so they run on Windows.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# ── Files that must never be line-ending normalized ─────────────────────────
# Git LFS pointer files, minified bundles, generated artifacts.
*.min.js        binary
*.min.css       binary
*.wasm          binary
*.pyc           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.tar.gz        binary

# ── Merge strategy for generated / multi-writer state files ────────────────────
# Every open PR on 2026-09-23 conflicted on these three files and nothing else
# (#1536, #1554, #1561): each session appends a tracker row and rewrites the
# graph report, so any two PRs collide. `union` keeps both sides' lines, which
# is exactly how those conflicts were being resolved by hand. The graph report
# is regenerated from source after any merge (Stop hook / rule 39), so either
# side will do; `graph-report` takes the incoming side. That driver needs
# `.claude/hooks/git-merge-drivers` to have run in the clone (SessionStart does
# it); without it git falls back to a normal text merge.
.claude/state/active-tasks.md merge=union
.claude/state/NEXT_ACTION.md merge=union
graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md merge=graph-report
